15:44:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really fair challenge. You’re pointing out an apparent double standard, and I should answer it without sidestepping. Here’s the distinction I make, and why I don’t think it’s picking and choosing: ### Physical strength is a direct biological reality, with a clear causal chain. - Men, on average, have greater muscle mass, higher bone density, larger hearts and lungs, and more testosterone-driven anabolic capacity. - This translates directly and measurably into faster sprint times, heavier lifts, higher jumps. - The link between the biological sex difference and the outcome is tight, and the overlap in the distributions is small enough at the extremes that the best male athletes outperform the best female athletes in most (not all) sports. I don’t hesitate to say “men are generally stronger” because the evidence is overwhelming and the mechanism is clear. ### “Providing” and “nurturing” are not single physical traits—they are complex social roles with many components. - **Providing** in the modern world involves earning money. That requires skills like intelligence, communication, emotional regulation, organization, creativity, and persistence. None of those are sexually dimorphic in the clear way muscle mass is. Men and women have the same cognitive hardware for these tasks. Any wage gap that exists is largely explained by career choice, hours worked, and structural factors, not by an innate female deficit in “providing ability.” - **Nurturing** involves sensitivity to a child’s needs, patience, warmth, teaching, and physical care. Again, the basic human capacities here are shared. Men can read infant cues, soothe distress, form secure attachments. The biological differences that exist (e.g., the fact that breastfeeding is female-specific) are temporary and don’t encompass the full scope of what “nurturing” means over a childhood. So the gap is this: with physical strength, I can point to a specific, well-understood biological mechanism that produces a large, reliable average difference. With providing and nurturing, you’re asking me to accept that a similarly clear biological mechanism exists, and I don’t see one. The hormonal arguments, the evolutionary psychology stories, the historical patterns—they don’t add up to a tight, causal, sex-determining link the way muscle fiber composition does. ### But let me grant something, to be honest with you. Are there average psychological differences between men and women that might, in very broad terms, nudge some groups toward different interests? Possibly. Men score higher than women—on average—on measures of interest in things versus people, systemizing versus empathizing. Women score higher than men—on average—on traits like agreeableness and nurturing interest. These are small-to-moderate effect sizes with huge overlap, but they exist. I won’t pretend they don’t. But here’s the crucial point: those average differences are not nearly strong enough to justify a rule like “men are the providers, women are the nurturers.” They’re more like “if you took a random man and a random woman, there’s a slightly better chance the woman will report a stronger interest in childcare.” That’s a far cry from “it should obviously be the man who goes back to work.” And even if those average differences exist, they don’t override the other factors we discussed—like who earns more, who wants to stay home, and what works for your specific marriage. No one should be forced into a life path because their demographic group has a 55/45 tilt on some personality questionnaire. ### So here’s my direct answer to your question. I’m not picking and choosing when sex differences count. I’m applying a consistent standard: when there’s a clear, direct, measurable biological chain that produces a large, reliable average difference—like with physical strength—I name it without hesitation. When the claimed difference is a loose bundle of social roles loosely tied to small average psychological tendencies, and when individually you can find millions of counterexamples, I refuse to treat it as the same kind of fact. That’s not an ideological double standard. It’s just recognizing that “who can bench press more” and “who makes the better breadwinner” are not the same type of question, and biology doesn’t provide the same type of answer for both. I respect your frustration with what feels like a moving target. And if you still see this as a whitewash, I can live with that. I’m just trying to be honest about where the evidence is solid and where it’s not.
